---
// THE CAPABILITY MATRIX. Every status on this page is GENERATED, never typed.
//
// The data comes from scripts/gen-capability-matrix.sh in the emulator repo, which runs the
// classifier and the conformance suite for real and records the command behind each block. If a
// row here is wrong, the machine is wrong — which is the only failure mode worth having, because
// it is the one a test can catch.
//
// This page exists because the hand-kept table in CURRENT-STATE.md has drifted from the machine
// twice that we know of, and both times it was found by accident. A published page maintained
// the same way would drift the same way, in front of customers.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/capabilities.json';

const title = 'What Fidelic can and cannot run — the capability matrix';
const description =
  'Generated from the classifier and the conformance suite, capability by capability, with the command behind every figure and the denominator behind every number.';

const shipped = data.capabilities.filter((c) => c.verdict === 'EXECUTE');
const refused = data.capabilities.filter((c) => c.verdict !== 'EXECUTE');
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="back"><a href="/">← Home</a></p>
    <h1>What Fidelic can and cannot run</h1>

    <p>
      Every status on this page is generated by running the classifier and the conformance
      suite. Nothing here is hand-written, and each block names the command that produced it,
      so you can run the same thing and get the same answer.
    </p>

    <p class="stamp">
      Generated {data.generated} from build <code>{data.build}</code>, against emulator image
      <code>{data.imageRef}</code> reporting <code>{data.imageVersion}</code>. Shipped tier:
      <code>{data.shippedTier}</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="vocabulary">The vocabulary</h2>
    <p>
      These words mean the same thing everywhere: on this page, in your
      <code>sfapex coverage</code> report, and in the emulator's own boot output. There is
      no second glossary.
    </p>
    <dl class="vocab">
      <dt><span class="v v-exec">EXECUTE</span></dt>
      <dd>Nothing in the reachable code is unsupported, so the interpreter will attempt it.
      <strong>This is not a claim that we ran it</strong> — that is what the fired log
      is for.</dd>
      <dt><span class="v v-sim">SIMULATED</span></dt>
      <dd>A qualifier on EXECUTE, never a verdict of its own. Real code runs, but some effect
      is deliberately modelled rather than performed. The divergence is named on the row.</dd>
      <dt><span class="v v-prov">Provisional</span></dt>
      <dd>Faithful behaviour whose response <em>envelope</em> still awaits confirmation against
      a real org. A different axis from SIMULATED: a capability can be neither, either, or both.</dd>
      <dt><span class="v v-ref">UNRESOLVED</span></dt>
      <dd>Declined by name, with a reason and a file and line. Not a crash, and not a silent
      wrong answer.</dd>
      <dt><span class="v v-rec">RECORDED-ONLY</span></dt>
      <dd>The code could run, but the event that starts it cannot happen locally — a
      platform-event subscriber, for instance. Nothing is claimed for it either way. It grades
      triggers rather than capabilities, so no row below carries it.</dd>
    </dl>

    <p class="denom">
      <strong>Two subjects, one vocabulary.</strong> The tables below grade
      <em>capabilities</em> — what this emulator supports at all. Your coverage report
      grades <em>your triggers</em>. They meet here: a capability marked UNRESOLVED is exactly
      what makes one of your triggers UNRESOLVED, and the refusal in your report names the
      capability that caused it.
    </p>

    <h2>Capabilities</h2>
    <p class="src">Produced by <code>{data.capabilitiesCommand}</code> — read from the same maps
      the classifier consults at runtime, so a capability cannot ship without its label.</p>

    <h3>Shipped — {shipped.length} of {data.capabilities.length}</h3>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Caveat</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {shipped.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{c.family}</code></td>
              <td>{c.note}</td>
              <td>
                <span class="v v-exec">EXECUTE</span>
                {c.simulated && <span class="v v-sim">SIMULATED</span>}
              </td>
              <td>{c.provisional ? <span class="caveat">{c.provisional}</span> : <span class="none">—</span>}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Not supported — {refused.length} of {data.capabilities.length}</h3>
    <p>Each of these is declined by name, per method, with a reason. Refusing beats returning a
      confident wrong answer: a refusal is visible and you can plan around it.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {refused.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{c.family}</code></td>
              <td>{c.note}</td>
              <td><span class="v v-ref">UNRESOLVED</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Conformance — {data.conformance.passed}/{data.conformance.total}</h2>
    <p class="src">Produced by <code>{data.conformance.command}</code></p>
    <p>
      Every test is tied to Salesforce's own documentation or to an observation from a real org.
      A test with no source does not go in.
    </p>
    <p class="denom"><strong>Denominator:</strong> {data.conformance.denominator}</p>

    <h2>Classification over a real codebase — {data.coverage.percent}%</h2>
    <p class="src">Produced by <code>{data.coverage.command}</code> against {data.coverage.corpus}</p>
    <p>
      {data.coverage.executePairs} of {data.coverage.pairs} trigger-context pairs classify
      EXECUTE, across {data.coverage.executeTriggers} of {data.coverage.triggers} triggers.
      This is a public, real Salesforce codebase, not a fixture we chose.
    </p>
    <p class="denom"><strong>Denominator:</strong> {data.coverage.denominator}</p>
    <p>
      The unit is the <strong>pair</strong>, not the trigger. A trigger declaring seven timings
      and a one-line before-insert trigger are not the same amount of an org, so counting
      triggers makes the figure swing on which <em>size</em> of trigger happens to be blocked.
      On this corpus the two framings differ by three points in our favour, which is why the
      harder one is published.
    </p>

    <h2>Reproduce this page</h2>
    <p>Point the analyzer at your own org and get the same shape of answer for your code:</p>
    <pre><code>docker run --rm -v "$PWD/mdapi":/mdapi:ro \
  --entrypoint /sfapex fidelic/emulator coverage --report /mdapi</code></pre>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .stamp{font-size:.85rem;color:#57606a;background:#f6f8fa;border-left:3px solid #89b4fa;
    padding:.6rem .85rem;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0}
  .src{font-size:.85rem;color:#57606a;margin-bottom:.4rem}
  .denom{font-size:.85rem;color:#24292f;background:#f6f8fa;padding:.6rem .85rem;border-radius:4px}
  .tablewrap{overflow-x:auto}
  table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.9rem;margin:.5rem 0 1.5rem}
  th,td{text-align:left;padding:.45rem .6rem;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d7de;vertical-align:top}
  th{font-size:.8rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.03em;color:#57606a}
  .v{display:inline-block;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace;font-size:.72rem;
    padding:.08rem .4rem;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;margin-right:.25rem}
  .v-exec{background:#dafbe1;color:#0a5a2a}
  .v-sim{background:#fff4d6;color:#7a5200}
  .v-prov{background:#e7e9ff;color:#33389a}
  .v-ref{background:#ffe9ec;color:#8b1d2c}
  .v-rec{background:#eef1f4;color:#44506b}
  .caveat{font-size:.82rem;color:#57606a}
  .none{color:#8c959f}
  .vocab dt{margin-top:.6rem}
  .vocab dd{margin:.15rem 0 .4rem 0;font-size:.92rem}
  @media (max-width:640px){ table{font-size:.82rem} }
</style>
